{"review_id":"00001788308111360583-0004098830-000003-cycle-review-2","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:8524acc807c011f9505fede6f6aeaec3596184dd621b52ed12c9403c2e64a1fe","findings":[{"finding_id":"xai-tool-result-turn-has-no-assistant-tool-calls","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"Tool-result turns are serialized with no preceding assistant `tool_calls` message","claim":"`wire_message` exhaustively matches only `MessageContent::Text` and `MessageContent::ToolResult`, emitting either `{\"role\": <role>, \"content\": ...}` or `{\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": ..., \"content\": ...}`. No branch can ever emit an assistant message carrying a `tool_calls` array, so on the second and later turns of a tool-using loop the request body contains a `role: \"tool\"` message whose `tool_call_id` refers to a call that appears nowhere in the transcript. The `/v1/chat/completions` surface this adapter targets requires each `role: \"tool\"` message to answer a directly preceding assistant message with matching `tool_calls`; without it the provider rejects the request. Every mock in the change matches on `method` and `path` only and never inspects the request body, so the multi-turn round-trip tests pass against a fake that would accept any transcript at all.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":215,"end":232}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":100,"end":135}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:6930a72dd30952b8102cc2957ae0422c210c5b5d17827c3a8f7f3e7cdab9dd10","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":33673,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:f0bef18978a06f5fb9985e4bb6a9b88933c5e05fa1910b2af44d08d4beedb539","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-6930a72dd30952b8102cc2957ae0422c210c5b5d17827c3a8f7f3e7cdab9dd10-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Reconstruct the assistant turn that issued the tool calls when building the request body — emit an assistant message with a `tool_calls` array whose ids match the following `tool_call_id` values — and pin it with a mock that matches on the request body of the second turn (asserting the assistant `tool_calls` entry precedes each `role: \"tool\"` message), so a transcript the provider would reject cannot pass.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-last-observed-state-is-adapter-wide","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"`last_resolved_model` / `last_cost_usd_ticks` are adapter-wide and misattribute under concurrent attempts","claim":"The provider-resolved model identity and the vendor `cost_in_usd_ticks` are stored as single `Mutex<Option<..>>` fields on `XaiAdapter` and overwritten by whichever `submit` last parsed a chunk. `InferenceAdapter::submit` takes `&self`, so one adapter instance can serve several attempts concurrently; a caller reading `last_resolved_model()` or `last_cost_usd_ticks()` after its own `submit` returns can observe another attempt's values, with no attempt id attached to distinguish them. That makes both values unsafe as the provider-resolved-identity and vendor-cost evidence the task's model-identity and billing criteria require them to feed, even though nothing consumes them yet.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":126,"end":142}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":165,"end":190}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"lint","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:6c36cc0a9482df633e18c25088e5090dc42c38e11f8f4b1132cc77529e3e4f0e","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":1268,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:f0bef18978a06f5fb9985e4bb6a9b88933c5e05fa1910b2af44d08d4beedb539","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-6c36cc0a9482df633e18c25088e5090dc42c38e11f8f4b1132cc77529e3e4f0e-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Return the resolved model identity and vendor cost as part of the per-attempt `SubmitOutcome` (or key the stored values by `AttemptId`) so each observation carries its own provider identity and cost, and add a test that runs two concurrent submits with different resolved models and asserts each caller sees its own.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-price-schedule-is-only-a-test-fixture","category":"maintainability_issue","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Grok price schedule exists only as an inline test fixture and a doc example","claim":"The versioned, dated Grok schedule (`calculation_version = \"xai-pricing-2026-09-01\"`, `grok-4.6` and `grok-4.3` rates) is constructed inline inside two `#[test]` functions in `crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs` and repeated as an example TOML block in `docs/contracts/xai-adapter.md`. No shipped configuration, default, or code path in the change makes that schedule available to an estimate, and the second test reimplements the accounting repository's per-category cost formula locally rather than calling it. The tests therefore assert that the literals match the literals, and no xAI worker's estimate can record this schedule as shipped.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":285,"end":400}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-workspace-advisory","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:9ccfd99399b018da9ed63b09c88e505e2011ef3657e1a68a962d1ec6d8259689","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":89045,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:f0bef18978a06f5fb9985e4bb6a9b88933c5e05fa1910b2af44d08d4beedb539","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-9ccfd99399b018da9ed63b09c88e505e2011ef3657e1a68a962d1ec6d8259689-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Ship the schedule where an estimate can consume it (a default or example price-schedule entry loaded by the accounting path) and rewrite the cost test to drive the real per-category estimate function with a Grok usage record instead of recomputing the formula inline.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-mocks-never-assert-request-body","category":"test_gap","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Deterministic fixtures assert responses only; the outbound request body is unverified","claim":"Every mock in `crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs` and `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/xai_adapter.rs` is mounted with `Mock::given(method(\"POST\")).and(path(\"/chat/completions\"))` and no body matcher; only one wire-level unit test (`bearer_token_header_is_sent_and_never_the_raw_env_name`) constrains anything the adapter sends. Nothing asserts that `stream_options.include_usage` is set, that tool definitions are serialized in the documented `{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{...}}` shape on the wire, that the `response_format` structured-output shape is emitted, or that the multi-turn transcript is well formed. The fixtures consequently cannot fail on any request-shape regression, including the tool-result transcript defect reported separately.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/xai_adapter.rs","range":{"start":95,"end":120}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":100,"end":125}}],"remediation":"Add body matchers (or capture and assert `server.received_requests()`) covering the streaming flags, the serialized tool definitions, the structured-output `response_format` block, and the full second-turn message sequence, so a change to the outbound wire shape fails a test.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-not-reachable-from-production","category":"maintainability_issue","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"xAI adapter still has no production caller wiring it to a configured worker","claim":"This cycle adds `build_xai_adapter_from_config` plus `XaiAdapterBuildError`, which does close the configuration-to-adapter gap as a callable function, and it is unit-tested from `RegistryWorkerConfig` / `[auth_profiles.*]` types. But nothing outside `#[cfg(test)]` calls it: the only non-test change to `familiar-ai-daemon` remains the `wiremock` dev-dependency, no registry or runtime dispatch selects `runtime = \"xai-api\"`, and `GROK_MODEL_FAMILY` and `XAI_CAPABILITY_PROFILE` in `crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/xai.rs` still have no non-test reader — the module comment continues to defer the authoritative capability record to operator-written configuration that this change neither reads nor validates. Selecting a Grok worker through a user-facing surface remains impossible, so the criterion that xAI is its own provider with runtime `xai-api` across routing, pricing, capability, and accounting paths still holds in tests and prose only.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/xai.rs","range":{"start":1,"end":80}},{"kind":"diff_hunk","path":"crates/familiar-ai-daemon/Cargo.toml","hunk":"@@ -57,3 +57,4 @@ predicates = \"3\"\n nix = { version = \"0.29\", features = [\"signal\"] }\n http-body-util = \"0.1\"\n tower = { version = \"0.5\", features = [\"util\"] }\n+wiremock.workspace = true"},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"lint","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:6c36cc0a9482df633e18c25088e5090dc42c38e11f8f4b1132cc77529e3e4f0e","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":1268,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:f0bef18978a06f5fb9985e4b