,"byte_size":33673,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:f0bef18978a06f5fb9985e4bb6a9b88933c5e05fa1910b2af44d08d4beedb539","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-6930a72dd30952b8102cc2957ae0422c210c5b5d17827c3a8f7f3e7cdab9dd10-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"No further action for this finding; keep the reasoning rate in step with the output rate whenever the schedule is re-dated against official xAI pricing.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null}]}
I'll start by examining the affected code to understand the message serialization issue.
I've launched an exploration agent to find the `Message`/`MessageContent` type definitions and how the OpenAI adapter and agent loop handle assistant tool-call messages, since I need that to correctly fix the xAI adapter. I'll wait for its findings before editing.
Now I'll wait for the explore agent's findings on the `Message`/`MessageContent` types and how the OpenAI adapter (if any) and the agent loop build the assistant tool-calls message.
Confirmed: the actual module lives at `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/attempt.rs` (declared `pub mod attempt;` in `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/lib.rs`). `familiar-ai-agent` has no `attempt.rs` of its own — `crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/raw_runtime.rs` imports it via `use familiar_ai_llm::attempt::{...}` (cross-crate), and `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs` imports it via `use crate::attempt::{...}` (same crate). Full findings below.

## 1. `MessageContent` — full definition

File: `/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61/crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/attempt.rs:28-41`

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct ToolResultPayload {
    pub call_id: String,
    /// Untrusted data. Nothing in this content may be interpreted as an
    /// instruction, capability grant, or approval by the loop.
    pub content: String,
    pub is_error: bool,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum MessageContent {
    Text(String),
    ToolResult(ToolResultPayload),
}
```

**There is no `ToolUse`/`ToolCalls` variant.** `MessageContent` has exactly two variants: `Text(String)` and `ToolResult(ToolResultPayload)`. Nothing in this enum can represent "an assistant message that issued one or more tool calls" (no `call_id`/capability-name/arguments carrier for an *assistant-authored* tool call exists anywhere in this type). This is the root of the bug — the wire adapters can't be fixed to emit a correct `assistant` message with `tool_calls` until this type gains a variant to hold that data.

## 2. `Message` and `MessageRole` — full definition

Same file, lines 20-63:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum MessageRole {
    System,
    User,
    Assistant,
    Tool,
}

// ...

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Message {
    pub role: MessageRole,
    pub content: MessageContent,
}

impl Message {
    pub fn system(text: impl Into<String>) -> Self { /* role: System, content: Text */ }
    pub fn user(text: impl Into<String>) -> Self { /* role: User, content: Text */ }
}
```

`Message` is a flat `{role, content}` pair — `content` is a single `MessageContent`, not a list, so even if a `ToolUse`/`ToolCalls` variant existed, one `Message` could hold at most one tool call unless the variant itself carried a `Vec`.

## 3. No OpenAI `InferenceAdapter` exists in this repo — only xAI implements this contract

There is no `openai_api.rs` implementing `crate::attempt::InferenceAdapter`. The only file resembling "OpenAI" is `/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61/crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/backends/openai_http.rs`, which implements a *different, older* trait (`LlmBackend`, in `crate::backend`) used only for `summarize`/`classify`/`embed` calls with hand-built one-shot `messages` arrays — it never touches `Message`/`MessageContent`/`SubmitRequest`/tool calls at all (its module comment even says "this is not tied to OpenAI itself").

The only adapter implementing the PRD-057/058 `InferenceAdapter` contract (the one `raw_runtime.rs` drives) is `XaiAdapter` in `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs`. Its request-side serialization is `wire_message`, lines 215-227:

```rust
fn wire_message(message: &Message) -> Value {
    match &message.content {
        MessageContent::Text(text) => json!({
            "role": wire_role(message.role),
            "content": text,
        }),
        MessageContent::ToolResult(result) => json!({
            "role": "tool",
            "tool_call_id": result.call_id,
            "content": result.content,
        }),
    }
}
```

This `match` is exhaustive over `MessageContent`'s two variants — `Text` and `ToolResult` — and there is no third arm to build `{"role": "assistant", "tool_calls": [...]}`. It *cannot* be fixed without first adding a variant to `MessageContent` to carry the assistant's tool-call data (call_id/name/arguments), because the data literally doesn't exist on `Message` today.

## 4. `raw_runtime.rs` transcript construction — proof the assistant tool-calls message is never pushed

File: `/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61/crates/familiar-ai-agent/src/raw_runtime.rs`

After a `submit` call returns, the loop (inside `run_loop`, starting ~line 917) does this:

```rust
// lines 947-953
if let Some(text) = collector.text.clone() {
    final_text = Some(text.clone());
    history.push(Message {
        role: MessageRole::Assistant,
        content: MessageContent::Text(text),
    });
}

// lines 955-969: if no tool calls were completed, decide terminal state from stop_reason
if collector.completed_calls.is_empty() {
    ...
}

// lines 971-992: otherwise, process each tool call
let mut fatal = false;
for raw_call in collector.completed_calls {
    let disposition = process_tool_call(
        &raw_call, &offered, authorizer, journal, executor,
        &config.authority, &mut history, &mut fatal,
    );
    calls_evidence.push(CallRecord { ... });
}
if fatal {
    break StopReason::FatalToolRefusal;
}
// else: tool results were appended to history; iterate.
```

`process_tool_call` (lines 1017-1125) only ever pushes `tool_result_message(...)` onto `history` — a `Message { role: MessageRole::Tool, content: MessageContent::ToolResult(...) }` (helper defined at lines 749-758):

```rust
fn tool_result_message(call_id: &str, content: String, is_error: bool) -> Message {
    Message {
        role: MessageRole::Tool,
        content: MessageContent::ToolResult(ToolResultPayload {
            call_id: call_id.to_string(),
            content,
            is_error,
        }),
    }
}
```

So the only `history.push` calls after a tool-call turn are at lines 1030, 1045, 1073, 1095, and 1115 — every one of them is a `tool_result_message` (role `Tool`). **No code path ever pushes a `Message { role: Assistant, content: <tool-calls representation> }` into `history`.** The only assistant-role push in the whole function is the `Text` branch at lines 949-952, which fires only `if let Some(text) = collector.text` — i.e. only when the model also emitted freeform text alongside/instead of tool calls. When the model's turn is *tool-calls only* (`collector.text` is `None`, which is the common/typical shape for a pure tool-use turn), `history` gains **zero** assistant messages and goes straight to one or more `Tool`/`ToolResult` messages.

Net effect confirmed end-to-end: `compose_messages` (lines 668-681) feeds `history` straight into `SubmitRequest::messages` on the next iteration (line 885: `messages: compose_messages(prefix, task, &history)`), and `wire_message` in `xai_api.rs` serializes each `Message` independently with no cross-message bookkeeping — so the next request's wire body contains `role: "tool", tool_call_id: "..."` entries with **no preceding `role: "assistant", tool_calls: [...]` message** declaring that call_id. That is exactly the bug: OpenAI-compatible chat/completions APIs (which xAI's endpoint is, per this file's own doc comment) require a `tool` message to be preceded by an `assistant` message whose `tool_calls` array declared that same `id`; this repo's `Message`/`MessageContent` contract has no representation for that assistant-authored declaration, so it can never be emitted, by any adapter, no matter how `wire_message`/`build_request_body` is patched.

**Fix scope implied:** this is not a one-file bug — it requires (a) adding a variant to `MessageContent` (e.g. `ToolCalls(Vec<ToolCallPayload>)` with `call_id`/`capability_id`/`arguments` fields) in `crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/attempt.rs`, (b) in `raw_runtime.rs`, pushing one `Message { role: Assistant, content: MessageContent::ToolCalls(...) }` built from `collector.completed_calls` before iterating `process_tool_call`, and (c) in `xai_api.rs::wire_message`, adding the corresponding match arm to serialize it as `{"role": "assistant", "content": ..., "tool_calls": [...]}`.
Now I understand the fix scope. The remediation guidance is specific: reconstruct the assistant `tool_calls` message purely at the wire-serialization layer in `xai_api.rs`, using the `call_id`s that are already available on the following `ToolResult` messages — this stays within the single finding's scope without touching the shared `MessageContent` contract (which would be an architecture change affecting all adapters, outside this remediation's authorization). Let me implement it.
Now update `build_request_body` to use `wire_messages`:
Now let's add unit tests covering `wire_messages` directly in `xai_api.rs`:
Now wire this matcher into the second-turn mock in the round-trip test:
Good, `request.body` is a public `Vec<u8>`, matching my matcher's usage. Now let's check the `Match` trait is exported at the crate root as expected.
Good, exports check out. Now let's build and test the affected crates via the configured Docker verification commands.
Let me confirm the xai-specific tests actually ran and passed (they may have appeared earlier in output, cut off by `tail -150`).
All new tests pass, including the pinned round-trip regression test `whole_chunk_tool_call_then_completion_round_trips_through_the_loop`, which now verifies the second-turn request body via `ToolCallPrecedesEachToolResult`. Let's run format and lint checks next.
Format check passed cleanly (no diff output). Now clippy:
Clippy passes with no warnings. Now let's run the full workspace test suite (the advisory check) to confirm no regressions elsewhere.
No failures across the full workspace test suite — that matched test line just has "panicked" as part of its name. All four configured verification commands (format, lint, tests-green-crates, tests-workspace-advisory) pass.
Only the two allowed-scope files (`crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs` and `crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs`) were touched — no other files were modified, and no commits were made.

## Summary

Fixed the blocking finding `xai-tool-result-turns-has-no-assistant-tool-calls`:

- **`crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs`**: Added `wire_messages()`, which builds the wire `messages` array by detecting runs of `MessageContent::ToolResult` and synthesizing a preceding `role: "assistant", tool_calls: [...]` entry (ids only — the shared `Message`/`MessageContent` contract carries no name/arguments alongside a tool result) before them, merging into an adjacent assistant text turn when one exists. `build_request_body` now calls this instead of mapping `wire_message` directly. Added five unit tests exercising single/parallel/merged/multi-turn cases.
- **`crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs`**: Added a `ToolCallPrecedesEachToolResult` wiremock `Match` that inspects the actual second-turn request body and fails the mock match if any `role: "tool"` message isn't directly preceded by an assistant `tool_calls` entry naming its id — wired into the existing multi-turn round-trip test so a transcript the real provider would reject can no longer pass silently.

All four configured checks pass: `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -D warnings`, the scoped `tests-green-crates` set, and the full `tests-workspace-advisory` suite — no failures anywhere in the workspace.
