{"review_id":"00001788308111360583-0004098830-000003-cycle-review-3","reviewed_manifest_hash":"sha256:2f77f049c0b607dced1c3d688073440a00dd54226762344e50f40d256a4d457c","findings":[{"finding_id":"xai-synthesized-tool-call-has-empty-function-name","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"Reconstructed assistant `tool_calls` entries carry an empty function name and placeholder arguments","claim":"`wire_messages` now inserts an assistant `tool_calls` message before each run of tool results, but every synthesized entry is emitted as `{\"id\": <call_id>, \"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"\", \"arguments\": \"{}\"}}` (xai_api.rs:262-266). The doc comment asserts that ids alone are \"what the wire format actually requires\", but that sufficiency claim is not supported by any cited xAI documentation in `docs/contracts/xai-adapter.md`, and OpenAI-compatible `/v1/chat/completions` implementations validate `messages[i].tool_calls[j].function.name` as a non-empty, pattern-constrained string, rejecting an empty name with a 400 on the very same class of request the earlier finding was about. Independently of validation, the transcript replayed to the model no longer states which capability each tool result answers or with what arguments, so on every second-and-later turn Grok sees results attached to nameless calls. The new `ToolCallPrecedesEachToolResult` matcher only checks that a preceding assistant message names the id (tests/xai.rs:128-133); it never inspects `function.name`, so the mock still accepts a body a real provider may reject.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":259,"end":269}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":107,"end":137}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:cd74c02b2b47e6683fa9e8706f09c1e70cfd9bcd290db5c98ed150eedfc1047c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":33583,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:829ccc979293ac8f495a28009e1f7b24860cef2a7736563f86945222b55362be","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-cd74c02b2b47e6683fa9e8706f09c1e70cfd9bcd290db5c98ed150eedfc1047c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Carry the assistant-issued tool call's `name` (and, if available, `arguments`) through the transcript type so the reconstructed `tool_calls` entry reproduces the real call rather than an empty placeholder — e.g. add the capability id to `ToolResultPayload` (or add an assistant tool-call message variant) and populate `function.name` from it. Until the ids-only shape is verified against official xAI documentation, extend the request-body matcher to assert a non-empty `function.name` matching the capability that produced each result, so a placeholder cannot pass the suite.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-price-schedule-tests-assert-only-their-own-literals","category":"test_gap","severity":"medium","blocking":false,"title":"Grok pricing and reasoning-token estimate tests assert locally constructed literals and a reimplemented formula, not shipped behavior","claim":"`grok_price_schedule_matches_official_xai_pricing_in_nanousd` builds a `PriceScheduleConfig` inside the test body and then asserts the same literals back (tests/xai.rs:347-411); it can never fail for a wrong or stale Grok rate, and no schedule with these rates is shipped in any code or configuration under the change, so nothing establishes that an `xai-api` worker's estimate actually uses Grok rates. `grok_estimate_covers_reasoning_tokens_not_just_output_tokens` then re-implements the cost formula in a local `category_cost` closure and asserts against itself, rather than invoking the `familiar-ai-storage` accounting path it claims to mirror — if that path ignored `reasoning_output_nanousd_per_million`, or applied a different per-category rule, both tests would still pass while reasoning tokens dropped out of every real Grok estimate. The contract document presents these as the tested evidence for the versioned/dated pricing requirement.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":347,"end":411}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":413,"end":455}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:cd74c02b2b47e6683fa9e8706f09c1e70cfd9bcd290db5c98ed150eedfc1047c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":33583,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:829ccc979293ac8f495a28009e1f7b24860cef2a7736563f86945222b55362be","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-cd74c02b2b47e6683fa9e8706f09c1e70cfd9bcd290db5c98ed150eedfc1047c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"Drive the estimate through the production accounting/estimating code (the `familiar-ai-storage` cost computation) with the Grok schedule as input, asserting the resulting estimate value for a usage record containing non-zero `reasoning_output_tokens`, instead of re-deriving the formula in the test. If the Grok schedule is intended to be operator configuration only, state that in the test's documentation and drop the self-asserting literal comparisons, which convey verification they do not perform.","status":"open","supersedes":null},{"finding_id":"xai-tool-result-turn-has-no-assistant-tool-calls","category":"correctness_defect","severity":"high","blocking":false,"title":"Tool-result turns lacked a preceding assistant tool_calls message","claim":"Addressed in this revision: `wire_messages` (crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs:238-292) now groups each run of `MessageContent::ToolResult` messages and emits an assistant message carrying a `tool_calls` array naming those ids directly before them, merging into a preceding assistant text turn where one exists. Unit tests (`tool_result_run_gets_a_preceding_assistant_tool_calls_message`, `parallel_tool_results_share_one_preceding_assistant_message_with_all_ids`, `assistant_text_turn_merges_with_the_synthesized_tool_calls_entry`, `multi_turn_transcript_never_leaves_a_tool_message_without_its_precursor`) and a body-inspecting wiremock matcher (`ToolCallPrecedesEachToolResult`) now assert the ordering the mocks previously ignored. The residual concern about the placeholder `function.name` in those synthesized entries is raised separately as `xai-synthesized-tool-call-has-empty-function-name`.","evidence":[{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-llm/src/xai_api.rs","range":{"start":229,"end":292}},{"kind":"file_range","path":"crates/familiar-ai-agent/tests/xai.rs","range":{"start":99,"end":137}},{"kind":"verification","check_id":"tests-green-crates","output":{"content_hash":"sha256:cd74c02b2b47e6683fa9e8706f09c1e70cfd9bcd290db5c98ed150eedfc1047c","media_type":"text/plain","byte_size":33583,"repository":"/home/trollboy/.local/state/familiar-ai/worktrees/drive-00001788303088789870-0004098830-000000/PRD-61","revision":"sha256:829ccc979293ac8f495a28009e1f7b24860cef2a7736563f86945222b55362be","storage_ref":"/home/trollboy/.local/share/familiar-ai/review-artifacts/sha256-cd74c02b2b47e6683fa9e8706f09c1e70cfd9bcd290db5c98ed150eedfc1047c-stdout","truncated":false,"omitted_bytes":0}}],"remediation":"No further action for this finding; track the placeholder function-name concern under xai-synthesized-tool-call-has-empty-function-name.","status":"resolved","supersedes":null}]}
